WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:05.020
Film Courage: Diego R. writes in “Specifically
for TV, how does one balance giving a satisfying

00:00:05.020 --> 00:00:11.419
season (or episode for that matter) plot and
closure with complete character arcs, while

00:00:11.419 --> 00:00:13.990
leaving room for a new season that might be
made?”

00:00:13.990 --> 00:00:19.680
Peter Russell: Well you don’t want to heal
the wounds, that’s basically it.

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:27.089
You take a show like Animal Planet (Animal
Kingdom?)

00:00:27.089 --> 00:00:34.890
Animal Kingdom which is a great show by John
Wells specializing now taking foreign shows

00:00:34.890 --> 00:00:36.900
and then turning them into American shows.&nbsp;

00:00:36.900 --> 00:00:46.660
You’ve got a matriarch (a poisonous matriarch)
Ellen Barkin and her kids who she’s completely,

00:00:46.660 --> 00:00:50.090
massively screwing up constantly.

00:00:50.090 --> 00:00:55.760
You know you just never want to have those
kids get better.

00:00:55.760 --> 00:00:56.760
And they don’t.

00:00:56.760 --> 00:01:01.690
One of them has just been killed and the others
are just as poisonous as they were, they’re

00:01:01.690 --> 00:01:03.390
a nest of snakes.

00:01:03.390 --> 00:01:12.610
I mean the great thing about television is
you replace healing which is what you get

00:01:12.610 --> 00:01:15.790
in a movie with surprise.

00:01:15.790 --> 00:01:24.200
So characters don’t change in television,
they surprise with new (often appalling) aspects

00:01:24.200 --> 00:01:26.100
to their character you didn’t know about.

00:01:26.100 --> 00:01:31.619
Let me ask you this, you probably have someone
you’ve known for 20 years right?

00:01:31.619 --> 00:01:33.890
And you know them better and better right?

00:01:33.890 --> 00:01:35.490
Film Courage: Uh…some of them.

00:01:35.490 --> 00:01:40.810
Peter: And as you get to know them better
and better don’t you find they’re insanely

00:01:40.810 --> 00:01:45.439
appalling people the more you know them?

00:01:45.439 --> 00:01:50.140
Film Courage: Uh…a few of them!

00:01:50.140 --> 00:01:52.770
Yes, that would be an understatement.

00:01:52.770 --> 00:01:54.440
Peter: Oh my God!

00:01:54.440 --> 00:01:55.690
Oh my God!

00:01:55.690 --> 00:01:58.610
They are even worse than I thought.&nbsp;

00:01:58.610 --> 00:01:59.869
Film Courage: Yep.

00:01:59.869 --> 00:02:01.119
Peter: That’s television.

00:02:01.119 --> 00:02:03.509
It’s like “Uh, Dave see you later.

00:02:03.509 --> 00:02:05.382
Glad you’re not with your mother anymore.”

00:02:05.382 --> 00:02:11.500
You’re learning new things about them…I’m
just using that as an example.

00:02:11.500 --> 00:02:13.930
Film Courage: He wasn’t the person I was
thinking of.&nbsp;

00:02:13.930 --> 00:02:18.140
Peter: You’re learning new things about
them that shock you.

00:02:18.140 --> 00:02:19.140
They’re surprising.

00:02:19.140 --> 00:02:20.140
Film Courage: Oh, yes.

00:02:20.140 --> 00:02:25.140
Peter: For instance in the SOPRANOS that we
were talking about earlier, Janice is getting

00:02:25.140 --> 00:02:33.020
married to a guy named Ritchie and they are
discussing their wedding plans one night and

00:02:33.020 --> 00:02:38.550
he gets angry at her and suddenly slaps her
which you’ve never, ever seen him do before.

00:02:38.550 --> 00:02:40.440
He’s never been violent to her.

00:02:40.440 --> 00:02:44.040
That’s a surprise that Ritchie suddenly
slaps Janice.

00:02:44.040 --> 00:02:48.420
It’s not a change in his character, he’s
not getting worse the way you do in a movie

00:02:48.420 --> 00:02:50.200
“I’m getting worse because of my wounds.”

00:02:50.200 --> 00:02:53.310
He’s just being Ritchie but he’s a surprise.

00:02:53.310 --> 00:03:00.120
Then Janice goes to a closet while Ritchie
while he’s sitting there eating his dinner

00:03:00.120 --> 00:03:05.250
and she comes out of the closet and she’s
got a gun.

00:03:05.250 --> 00:03:06.250
And he goes “C’mon?”

00:03:06.250 --> 00:03:07.250
And BOOM!

00:03:07.250 --> 00:03:08.250
She shoots him.

00:03:08.250 --> 00:03:09.700
Right at the table, okay?

00:03:09.700 --> 00:03:12.120
That’s a new surprise from Janice.

00:03:12.120 --> 00:03:16.840
You haven’t seen her do that before, right.

00:03:16.840 --> 00:03:18.720
Is she healing or getting worse?

00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:20.190
No she’s just Janice.

00:03:20.190 --> 00:03:24.370
It’s a new revelation of character.

00:03:24.370 --> 00:03:32.060
So the way you do it in television is you
keep surprising us with new aspects of a character.

00:03:32.060 --> 00:03:35.260
Someone reveals something about themselves
you didn’t know before.

00:03:35.260 --> 00:03:36.880
That’s why television is great.

00:03:36.880 --> 00:03:39.370
It’s more like real life than movies, right?

00:03:39.370 --> 00:03:43.810
It’s just you and your friends learning
more and more appealing things about each

00:03:43.810 --> 00:03:46.560
other as you go along.

00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:49.350
And they are not things that are going to
make you go “Oh, they’re healing or they’re

00:03:49.350 --> 00:03:50.350
getting better.”

00:03:50.350 --> 00:03:52.990
It’s like “That’s just horrible.”

00:03:52.990 --> 00:03:59.450
And so that’s the big difference between
movies and television, surprise replaces healing.

